### 4. C2 Beaconing Detection (Statistical)

**ATT&CK Technique:** T1071 — Application Layer Protocol / T1095 — Non-Application Layer Protocol  
**Log Source:** Zeek network logs (`zeek_conn`)

```spl
index=network sourcetype=zeek_conn
| bucket _time span=1h
| stats count by _time, dest_ip, dest_port
| eventstats avg(count) as avg_count, stdev(count) as stdev_count by dest_ip
| eval z_score=abs((count - avg_count) / stdev_count)
| where z_score < 1 AND count > 20
```

**What it detects:** Statistically regular outbound connections to the same destination — the hallmark of automated C2 beaconing. Human browsing is irregular; malware callbacks are not.

**Logic:** A low z-score means the connection count per hour barely varies from the average — that regularity is the signal. Combined with a volume filter (`count > 20`), this reduces noise from legitimate low-frequency cron jobs.

**True positive indicators:** Regular intervals (e.g. every 60s), unusual destination ports, connections to newly registered domains, no corresponding DNS queries.

